  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Cesar Perdomo gave them five innings of two-run ball. The offense never answered.",
    "what_this_means": "Shay Whitcomb drove in both Giants runs, but five hits and seven strikeouts is not a recipe for winning games you're down by two in the ninth. They were tied through six. The game was there to take. They didn't take it. The bullpen held clean through eight innings, but the bats stayed quiet when it mattered most. This is what happens when a team has chances but doesn't convert them. They left runners on in the third and ninth, scored once with runners in scoring position across six at-bats, and watched Minnesota's lead grow in the seventh because the offense couldn't answer back. A starter pitching that well deserves more than two runs. The Giants gave him nothing to work with in the final three innings. Down 32 games in the division already, they can't afford to waste nights like this. Five hits and a loss is a wasted opportunity made routine.",
    "what_to_watch": "Tarik Skubal takes the mound tonight for the Dodgers with a 2.85 ERA. One of the sharpest starters in baseball at a time when the Giants' lineup is hitting .246 and can't string together consecutive at-bats.",
